course: dictionary
lesson: 13369
title: Haste: when hurrying goes wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Haste is a noun. It means great speed, especially when you hurry, and it often means too much speed. «In her haste, she forgot her bag.» She went so fast that she left something behind, so haste caused a mistake.
explain: You may also meet haste in the phrase «make haste», which is old-fashioned. It means hurry up. «Make haste, or we will miss the bus!» tells you that the bus will not wait, so you need to move quickly.
explain: Look at the words around haste to find its meaning. In «The cat knocked the cup off the table in its haste», the cup fell over because the cat moved too fast. The clues show that haste is speed that leads to accidents.
fact: Haste is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «great haste», not «a haste».
fact: Haste means great speed, often too much speed, when you hurry.
fact: «In haste» means quickly and without much care.
fact: «More haste, less speed» is a proverb: rushing can make you slower in the end, because mistakes take time to fix.
fact: Haste near roads is dangerous: always stop, look and listen before you cross.
exercise: «In her haste, she forgot her bag.» What does haste mean? | A kind of bag | *Great speed, often too much speed | Sleeping late | Slow and careful thinking
exercise: «Make haste, or we will miss the bus!» What does «make haste» mean? | Sit down quietly | *Hurry up | Ask for help | Stop and rest
exercise: Why is crossing the road in haste dangerous? | Because roads are too wide to cross | Because it makes the cars go slower | *Because you may not look or listen properly for cars | Because the road is always closed
test: The proverb «More haste, less speed» means that | *rushing can make you slower in the end | haste is a kind of sport | going fast is always the best way | you should never speed up
test: What is another word for haste in «He ran with great haste to catch the train»? | quiet | rest | *speed | sleep
exam: Which sentence shows haste causing a mistake? | He read his book slowly and carefully | She sat quietly and waited for her turn | *He dropped the eggs because he rushed to the kitchen | We walked to school together on a sunny day
